  "problem": "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is a negative number.\n\nII. $q$ is a multiple of $3.$\n\nIII. $p$ is an irrational number.",
  "worked_solution": "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $q$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $p$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
